1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:06,999
Planning a walk on Mars should start with
evidence, not guesswork.

2
00:00:07,049 --> 00:00:08,688
This is Mars Explorer —

3
00:00:08,738 --> 00:00:12,802
a mission console built entirely on published
NASA data.

4
00:00:14,697 --> 00:00:19,955
Imam Hosen, our team lead, architected and
built the entire system —

5
00:00:20,005 --> 00:00:24,092
the console, the retrieval engine, and the AI
agents.

6
00:00:24,942 --> 00:00:31,072
Ahad, our researcher and NASA data analyst,
sourced and verified every dataset.

7
00:00:31,922 --> 00:00:36,234
And Biswadev, our 3D and UI designer and QA
tester, shaped the visuals and tested every

8
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:40,597
feature.

9
00:00:41,947 --> 00:00:44,341
The home page opens on real ground —

10
00:00:44,391 --> 00:00:47,803
HiRISE imagery draped over NASA's elevation
model —

11
00:00:47,853 --> 00:00:51,266
and lets you ask the mission a question right
away.

12
00:00:53,661 --> 00:00:58,800
In the console, switch between NASA Mars Trek
layers, then scrub through the sols to watch

13
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:03,990
Perseverance's published path unfold across
Jezero.

14
00:01:06,385 --> 00:01:09,795
Load a real route, and the system samples the
terrain.

15
00:01:09,845 --> 00:01:13,575
The Traverse Risk Index lists every component
and its source, and the profile shows

16
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:17,355
elevation and slope along the way.

17
00:01:19,749 --> 00:01:25,505
Pick two points, and an A* search suggests a
slope-aware corridor —

18
00:01:25,555 --> 00:01:28,104
always labeled non-certifying.

19
00:01:30,499 --> 00:01:35,610
Switch to 3D, and fly the route across real
HiRISE imagery.

20
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:40,860
The assistant answers with cited passages.

21
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:44,836
A command palette puts every action one
keystroke away, and a single click turns the

22
00:01:44,886 --> 00:01:48,813
route into a mission briefing.

23
00:01:51,208 --> 00:01:54,340
The research console hosts the mission agent.

24
00:01:54,390 --> 00:01:57,910
A fast router picks tools in one pass, they
run in parallel, and the answer streams in,

25
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:01,481
step by step.

26
00:02:03,876 --> 00:02:09,329
Ask the corpus, and every sentence comes back
with a clickable citation.

27
00:02:09,379 --> 00:02:12,920
The live panel previews retrieval as you type
—

28
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:15,137
keyword, semantic, or hybrid.

29
00:02:17,532 --> 00:02:21,342
Add a page or a document, and it's indexed in
seconds.

30
00:02:21,392 --> 00:02:27,060
The quality table and the 3D embedding space
show exactly how evidence is found.

31
00:02:29,455 --> 00:02:31,255
Earth gets its own lane.

32
00:02:31,305 --> 00:02:36,805
NASA's EONET feed shows live wildfires and
storms with full provenance —

33
00:02:36,855 --> 00:02:39,812
fetch time, content hash, and sources —

34
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:41,662
on a separate Earth map.

35
00:02:44,057 --> 00:02:46,644
Every page is a 3D data story.

36
00:02:46,694 --> 00:02:49,193
Earth and Mars at true scale.

37
00:02:49,243 --> 00:02:52,094
Jezero's elevation, cell by cell.

38
00:02:52,144 --> 00:02:55,610
Perseverance's traverse, stacked by sol.

39
00:02:58,005 --> 00:03:02,274
Where Earth and Mars are on any day, with the
light delay.

40
00:03:02,324 --> 00:03:04,657
Every NASA landing on one globe.

41
00:03:04,707 --> 00:03:07,636
And the source stack behind every claim.

42
00:03:10,031 --> 00:03:13,647
Perseverance's climb, in published elevations.

43
00:03:13,697 --> 00:03:15,719
Slopes in measured relief.

44
00:03:15,769 --> 00:03:22,892
A route raised into an elevation curtain, a
server PDF, and a mission card ready to share.

45
00:03:25,287 --> 00:03:27,483
Traces record every assistant run.

46
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:31,216
The pipeline page shows what the system holds,
and the architecture view animates how NASA

47
00:03:31,266 --> 00:03:34,948
data flows through it.

48
00:03:37,343 --> 00:03:40,181
And for the story around the science:

49
00:03:40,231 --> 00:03:46,657
a NASA image gallery, model comparisons, Earth
analog questions, and a guided tour.

50
00:03:49,052 --> 00:03:50,425
Real NASA data.

51
00:03:50,475 --> 00:03:51,753
Honest limits.

52
00:03:51,803 --> 00:03:53,840
Answers you can trace.

53
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:55,263
Mars Explorer —

54
00:03:55,313 --> 00:03:57,350
from Binary Explorers.
